---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import Scroll from "../components/Scroll.astro";
import Evidence from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Evidence.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import living from "../data/living.json";
import { nCharts, nSolid } from "../lib/people.js";
---
<Base
  title="Method"
  eyebrow="How to read this archive"
  dek="Every claim on this site carries its confidence. A thing that is documented says so; a thing that is family tradition says that too, and is kept anyway, because pretending otherwise is how family myths become family history.">


  <section class="blk" id="ladder">
    <h2>The five words</h2>
    <p class="prose">All seven of these family archives grade a claim with the same five words, so a
    level means the same thing wherever you meet it. The word is always printed next to the colour,
    because a reader should never have to know the palette to know the confidence.</p>
    <dl class="ladder">
      <dt><Evidence level="documented" /></dt>
      <dd>A record was read, and it says this. The source is named.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="probable" /></dt>
      <dd>The evidence points one way and nothing found so far points the other. Not proven.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="inferred" /></dt>
      <dd>Reasoned from what is known — a gap of the right size, a name in the right place — rather
      than witnessed in a record.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="family" /></dt>
      <dd>Told, remembered, or written on a family chart. Kept, because discarding it would lose the
      lead; labelled, because it is not evidence.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="disputed" /></dt>
      <dd>Two sources disagree and neither has been ruled out. Both are shown.</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="notclaimed" style="border-left-color:var(--accent)">
      <span class="lab">And what a calendar is</span>
      <p class="prose">A great deal of this archive's medieval evidence is not the document. It is a
      <strong>calendar</strong>: a summary of a deed, made by an archivist who had the parchment in
      front of them, and printed in a catalogue. Nearly everything on
      <a href={u("/swonhungre/")}>the name in its own century</a> is of that kind.</p>
      <p class="prose">It sits between <span class="chip doc">documented</span> and
      <span class="chip inf">inferred</span> and this archive grades it <strong>documented</strong>,
      for one reason: a calendar is a reading by somebody competent, working from the original, whose
      job was to describe it accurately — which is a different thing from a conclusion. What it is
      not is the deed. A calendar can mis-transcribe a name, compress a clause, or date a document
      wrongly: <strong>Berkeley Castle dates BCM/A/1/24/173 to the fifteenth century and the printed
      translation of the same transaction dates it 1329</strong>, two hundred years apart. Where a
      page rests on a calendar it says so, and where a calendar and a printed text disagree, both are
      shown.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="notclaimed">
      <span class="lab">Not claimed</span>
      <p class="prose">Some pages carry this instead. It states the thing a reader is likely to
      assume — the connection that would be tidy, the identification that almost works — and says
      plainly that this archive does not assert it. An archive is judged as much by what it refuses
      to say as by what it says.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>How a person here gets graded</h2>
    <p class="prose">The five words above are not applied by hand, person by person. Every one of the
    {nCharts} people in this archive is graded from <span class="mono">provenance.json</span>, which
    records what has actually been read:</p>
    <ul class="prose" style="margin-top:.8rem">
      <li><strong>Documented</strong> — at least one record names the relationship: a Queensland
      registration, a baptism, a passenger record, a sworn deposition. <strong>33 people.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Disputed</strong> — they hang off one of the two grafts. 71 reach the archive only
      through the <a href={u("/hornby/")}>Hornby claim</a>, a single unevidenced link; 27 only
      through the Keele link, which was looked for in the right registers and
      <a href={u("/how-far-back/")}>found to be wrong</a>. <strong>98 people.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Family</strong> — on the family tree, with nothing found for them either way.
      <strong>342 people.</strong> This is most of the archive, and saying so is the point.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">Which means the proportion is visible rather than
    implied: <strong>seven per cent</strong> of the people here are proved by a record. The rest is
    a tree that has not been tested yet, and a hundred people hanging off two links that have.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="blk">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">The five labels</h2>
    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><span class="chip doc">documented</span></td>
              <td>A named source with a reference, and where possible the scan itself.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="chip inf">inferred</span></td>
              <td>A reasoned conclusion drawn from documented facts, with the reasoning written out
                  in full so that it can be overturned by anyone who finds it wanting.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="chip dis">superseded</span></td>
              <td>Asserted in the family record, and now displaced by a document that says otherwise.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="chip dis">disputed</span></td>
              <td>Asserted in the family record but unsupported, or contradicted, by what can be seen.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="chip lore">family lore</span></td>
              <td>Told, remembered, not corroborated. Kept because it is precious; labelled because
                  pretending otherwise is how family myths become family history.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" id="living">
    <h2>Living people</h2>
    <p class="lede"><strong>A living person (80 or younger, with no death recorded) is named with
    their relationships and their town and country, and nothing more.</strong> A name, a
    relationship — wife, husband, daughter, brother — and the town and country the tree gives for them.
    No date, no record, no source, no note, no occupation, no photograph, and no structured data for a
    search engine to harvest. Their page is marked <em>noindex</em>, which keeps it out of the sitemap
    and out of the search index.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Who is living is decided by one definition</strong>, written once for
    every archive in the family (the shared kit's <span class="mono">living_rule.py</span>), and not by this
    archive's own test. A person is living if they were born 80 years ago or less — by year, this year
    minus the year of birth — and the tree records no death or burial for them. A recorded death or
    burial, dated or not, means dead. A <strong>public source</strong> means not living: a citation from
    a public record collection (FamilySearch, MyHeritage's record collections, Findmypast and the others
    on the shared list) is the evidence that a person is no longer private, and what that source says may
    be shown. A private family-tree entry — a GEDCOM, a tree profile on another site, family lore —
    does not count. And a person with <strong>no birth year</strong> cannot be shown to be 80 or younger,
    so is not living by this test, which is what lets the fourteenth century be a list of people and not
    a list of names with a town.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">That replaced a stricter-looking rule that was weaker where it mattered:
    until 16 October 2026 this archive inferred an upper bound on everyone's birth year through the
    family graph and called someone dead only when that bound was more than a hundred years back.
    That made people aged 81 to 100 with no death recorded &ldquo;living&rdquo;, and gave no weight to a public
    source. It is gone. What is left is stated on the person's own record, and the arithmetic is
    checked on every build by a second implementation that shares none of the generator's code.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Every person this archive names has a page of their own.</strong> The
    direct line and the thirteen families have pages, and so do the parents, brothers and sisters and
    spouses they are shown with; a relative's own relatives are listed on their page only where they have
    a page already, so the naming stops there. The tree holds thousands of people this archive never
    names, and they stay unpublished.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">On this build the rule judges <strong>{living.counts.living.toLocaleString()}</strong> of the {living.counts.tree.toLocaleString()}
    people in the tree to be living. <strong>{living.published.withPage}</strong> of them have a page
    here ({living.published.onDirectLine} are on the direct line, and
    {living.published.livingRelativesWithPage} are only named beside a relative). Of the
    {living.published.withPage} with a page, {living.published.withTown} carry a town (and a country
    where the tree gives one), {living.published.countryOnly} a country only, and {living.published.noPlace}
    no place at all. The place is the place of birth, reduced to a town and a country: a street, a
    hospital or a county is dropped, and the tree records no residence for anybody living.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>{living.published.heldOut.total} people are left out
    altogether</strong>, not named even as somebody's relative. One is the person whose DNA result
    the <a href={u("/dna/")}>DNA page</a> describes, who is not named there or anywhere. The rest are
    people who <em>might be children</em>: not recorded dead, no public source, no birth year, and a
    living parent. With no year there is no telling a child from an adult, and a child is not named.
    <strong>A child whose birth year is known is not left out</strong>: they follow the same standard
    as any living person, a name, relationships, a town and a country, a page and no date. The rule is applied
    across the whole tree ({living.published.heldOut.possibleMinorsInTree} people meet it); this build
    met {living.published.heldOut.possibleMinors} of them while looking for relatives. A page shows
    that count and never who.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>A removal request is honoured within days</strong>, without
    argument and without requiring a reason.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem">And the rule is checked, not just stated</h3>
    <p>A rule applied in code is only as good as the proof that it held. Every build runs
    <span class="mono">tools/check_living.py</span>, which reads the <strong>built HTML</strong> — the
    only place a leak can actually be seen — together with the tree, and refuses to ship if any date,
    place, record, source, note or occupation belonging to a living person appears on a page or in a
    file the site serves, if a living person's page is not <em>noindex</em>, if one is in the sitemap
    or the search index, if a living person carries structured data, if the tree and the generator
    disagree about who is living, if someone is published whom the archive does not name, or if a name
    appears with no page behind it. It fails the build rather
    than warning, and it fails if it cannot read the tree, because a guard that cannot check has not
    passed. The part that needs only the committed data and the built pages
    (<span class="mono">tools/check_living_standard.py</span>) also runs in the deploy.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>It does not work from a list of living people, and that is
    deliberate.</strong> A file naming two thousand living relatives, committed to a public
    repository, would be exactly the disclosure this rule exists to prevent. The tree is read at
    check time from the GEDCOM, which is never committed. What <em>is</em> committed is the
    declaration: the standard, and the exceptions.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>Six living people are named on purpose, in prose, and that is written down rather than
      waved through.</strong> Three are the children of Ken D'Arcy, credited as the authors of the
      eulogy that is this archive's richest single source; two are the couple named in the sentence
      that explains why the archive exists; one is the living generation the direct line runs down to.
      That is a standing decision of the archive's owner, affirmed on 14 and 15 September 2026 when the
      question was put directly, and it is recorded alongside the names so that it is not quietly
      reversed later by somebody reading the rule and not the reason. Each is one line in
      <span class="mono">site/src/data/living.json</span>. It is unchanged by the standard above, and
      it is not what lets anybody else be named: every living person is named, with a page of their
      own.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Where the data comes from, and what that means</h2>
    <p>The spine of this archive is a MyHeritage family tree of {living.counts.tree.toLocaleString()} people, exported to GEDCOM and
    parsed here. That is a <em>starting point and not an authority.</em> An online tree records what
    somebody believed; this archive's job is to say which of those beliefs survive contact with a
    document.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">So the tree is treated as a witness rather than a source. Where it
    carries a citation, the citation is shown on the person's page and marked as
    <em>attached in the tree</em> rather than <em>checked here</em>. Where this archive has gone to
    the original, the page says so.</p>
    <Note>
      The most useful thing this method produces is not a longer line. It is a
      <strong>shorter, truer one</strong>, with the join that fails clearly marked, so that the next
      person to work on it starts where the evidence actually stops rather than where the chart does.
      <a href={u("/hornby/")}>The worked example →</a>
    </Note>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>How to read a pedigree chart on this site</h2>
    <p class="dek">Every person page opens with one, and the border down the left edge of each card
    says <strong>how that relationship is known</strong> — nothing else. It is the only thing the
    border encodes, because conflating "this is my ancestor" with "this is proved" is how a pedigree
    starts lying.</p>

    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>The border</th><th>Means</th><th>The card also says</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Solid</strong></td>
              <td>the link is written down in a record this archive has opened and read</td>
              <td>which record — a registration number, a file reference, a deposition</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Dotted</strong></td>
              <td>the link comes from the family tree and has not been tested</td>
              <td><span class="chip inf">tree</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Dashed, name struck through</strong></td>
              <td>the link comes from the tree <em>and</em> sits above one of the two joins this
                  archive has tested and rejected</td>
              <td>which graft — Hornby or Keele</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>

    <Note>
      <strong>The count under each chart is the point of the chart.</strong> It says how many of the
      relationships drawn are written in a record and how many are not.
      <strong>Of {nCharts} charts on this site, {nSolid} contain a solid edge.</strong> A reader who takes
      nothing else from this archive should take that: a family tree is a set of assertions, and
      until somebody opens a document, that is all it is.
      <a href={u("/register/")}>The register, where the same split is counted →</a>
    </Note>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2 id="question-nobody-asked">A correct answer to a question nobody asked</h2>
    <p class="dek">This archive's oldest rule is that <strong>a null must carry a control</strong> — a test
    that could have failed and did not. It is written entirely about zeroes, and on 21 September 2026 it
    became clear that is only half the problem. Three faults were found here in one day and <strong>none of
    them returned a zero.</strong></p>
    <ul class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">A sweep of a catalogue <strong>returned a hundred records</strong> and
      printed the true count of a hundred and twenty-six beside them. The number was right and it was the
      number of records fetched, not the number that existed.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">A spelling table <strong>returned 371</strong>. That was the count of
      index entries, and the page called them baptisms; one row in six is a repeat.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">A map <strong>said a pin was exact</strong>. The field recorded that the
      lookup had succeeded, not that it had answered the question, so Berkeley spent weeks on the geocode for
      the word England with six people on it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>An instrument can be working perfectly and be pointed at
    something else.</strong> What it reports is then true, and about the wrong thing, and it arrives wearing
    exactly the face of an answer. A nil at least announces itself as bad news worth checking. A confident
    yes does not, which is why each of the three above survived a commit message, a published page and, in
    one case, a gate.</p>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">So the rule is put a second way, and this one can be run rather
    than admired: <strong>if the thing I am looking for were definitely there, could this instrument have told
    me?</strong> Ask a filter for something already visible on the page in front of you. Ask a comparison to
    fail on a difference you planted. Ask a generator to survive a rebuild rather than a re-read. The
    operational form is the Booyzen archive's, arrived at from three faults of its own the same afternoon —
    a comparison that iterated dictionary keys and could not fail, a place filter returning nought for a place
    printed in its own results, and a confidence field reporting on the wrong thing.</p>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Three of this archive's faults were found that
    way in a single afternoon, and all three were in its own tools</strong> — a stopper that selected
    nothing, an exit trap that reported a clean pass over a build that had been killed, and a check asked
    about a file it does not read. That they were all tools is not chance. A claim about a family arrives
    from outside and is graded on the way in; the world can contradict it. <strong>A claim about an
    instrument is a claim about oneself</strong> — my filter filtered, my trap trapped — written by the one
    person who needs it to be true, and it crosses no boundary where anything would check it. The boundary
    on 21 September was a neighbouring archive describing its own work in enough detail that the shape was
    recognisable from here. That only happens if both sides write up what they got wrong at the same length
    as what they found.</p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p class="dek">A correction is the most valuable thing anyone can send this archive. If something here is
    wrong — a date, a place, a join, a person who should not be published — say so, and it will be
    changed and the change recorded.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
